{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"7ee4d8ebf3a00bb5","name":"card_event_saar_probe","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/France/events/card_event_saar_probe","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"SAAR OFFENSIVE","text":"Deal 7 damage to target unit. Add CROSS OF LORRAINE to hand if this is the first order given this turn.","flavorText":"The Saar Offensive was a military operation by the French Army in German territory, which took place between September 7th and October 17th, 1939, at the beginning of the “Phoney War.” After Germany invaded Poland on September 1st, 1939, the Allies declared war on Germany on September 3rd. Four days later, the French Army crossed the border between Moselle and Saarland. The civilians had already been evacuated, and a large number of anti-personnel and anti-tank mines had been deployed. As soon as the French arrived, the Germans detonated the bridges. Initially, they offered little resistance and chose to retreat behind the Siegfried Line. Only a few skirmishes took place, resulting in minimal casualties. The French advanced 8 kilometers into German territory and stopped 4 kilometers short of the Siegfried Line, staying out of reach of German heavy artillery. After Soviet forces invaded Poland on September 17th, French operations halted, and the frontline was fortified. The order to retreat to the Maginot Line was given on September 21st. Following Poland’s defeat, German divisions were transferred to the Western Front, allowing the German 1st Army to launch a counter-offensive into French territory from October 16th to 24th.In the end, the Saar Offensive and the subsequent German counterattack resulted in approximately 2,000 French casualties and 700 German casualties. The situation then remained unchanged until May 10th, 1940. This operation is sometimes considered a missed opportunity for the French to defeat Germany at a time when it was vulnerable and before the conflict escalated into a full-scale European war.","faction":"France","type":"order","rarity":"Uncommon","cost":6,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_saar_probe","title":{"Namespace":"card_france","Key":"card_event_saar_probe_title","SourceString":"SAAR OFFENSIVE","LocalizedString":"SAAR OFFENSIVE"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::France","kredits":6,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_france","Key":"card_event_saar_probe_text","SourceString":"Deal 7 damage to target unit. Add CROSS OF LORRAINE to hand if this is the first order given this turn.","LocalizedString":"Deal 7 damage to target unit. Add CROSS OF LORRAINE to hand if this is the first order given this turn."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_france","Key":"card_event_saar_probe_history","SourceString":"The Saar Offensive was a military operation by the French Army in German territory, which took place between September 7th and October 17th, 1939, at the beginning of the “Phoney War.” After Germany invaded Poland on September 1st, 1939, the Allies declared war on Germany on September 3rd. Four days later, the French Army crossed the border between Moselle and Saarland. The civilians had already been evacuated, and a large number of anti-personnel and anti-tank mines had been deployed. As soon as the French arrived, the Germans detonated the bridges. Initially, they offered little resistance and chose to retreat behind the Siegfried Line. Only a few skirmishes took place, resulting in minimal casualties. The French advanced 8 kilometers into German territory and stopped 4 kilometers short of the Siegfried Line, staying out of reach of German heavy artillery. After Soviet forces invaded Poland on September 17th, French operations halted, and the frontline was fortified. The order to retreat to the Maginot Line was given on September 21st. Following Poland’s defeat, German divisions were transferred to the Western Front, allowing the German 1st Army to launch a counter-offensive into French territory from October 16th to 24th.In the end, the Saar Offensive and the subsequent German counterattack resulted in approximately 2,000 French casualties and 700 German casualties. The situation then remained unchanged until May 10th, 1940. This operation is sometimes considered a missed opportunity for the French to defeat Germany at a time when it was vulnerable and before the conflict escalated into a full-scale European war.","LocalizedString":"The Saar Offensive was a military operation by the French Army in German territory, which took place between September 7th and October 17th, 1939, at the beginning of the “Phoney War.” After Germany invaded Poland on September 1st, 1939, the Allies declared war on Germany on September 3rd. Four days later, the French Army crossed the border between Moselle and Saarland. The civilians had already been evacuated, and a large number of anti-personnel and anti-tank mines had been deployed. As soon as the French arrived, the Germans detonated the bridges. Initially, they offered little resistance and chose to retreat behind the Siegfried Line. Only a few skirmishes took place, resulting in minimal casualties. The French advanced 8 kilometers into German territory and stopped 4 kilometers short of the Siegfried Line, staying out of reach of German heavy artillery. After Soviet forces invaded Poland on September 17th, French operations halted, and the frontline was fortified. The order to retreat to the Maginot Line was given on September 21st. Following Poland’s defeat, German divisions were transferred to the Western Front, allowing the German 1st Army to launch a counter-offensive into French territory from October 16th to 24th.In the end, the Saar Offensive and the subsequent German counterattack resulted in approximately 2,000 French casualties and 700 German casualties. The situation then remained unchanged until May 10th, 1940. This operation is sometimes considered a missed opportunity for the French to defeat Germany at a time when it was vulnerable and before the conflict escalated into a full-scale European war."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Uncommon","selectTargetOnPlayedFromHand":true,"cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::BloodAndIron","usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnEndOfTurn"],"spawnCardName":"card_event_cross_of_lorraine","soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'War_UI_Drum_Movement_Field_Snares_3'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/War_UI/Waves/Drum_Movements/War_UI_Drum_Movement_Field_Snares_3.0"},"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/France/Mini_Ally_Expansion_25/t_saar_probe.t_saar_probe","SubPathString":""},"artist":"Volmi Games","historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/SAAR_OFFENSIVE.SAAR_OFFENSIVE","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":360.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_france","Key":"card_event_saar_probe_photo","SourceString":"French soldiers in Lauterbach, Saarland, Germany, September 1939.","LocalizedString":"French soldiers in Lauterbach, Saarland, Germany, September 1939."},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_AttackOrder.BP_AttackOrder_C","SubPathString":""}},"variables":[{"name":"orderPlayed","type":"bool","direction":"in"}],"functions":[{"name":"CanPlayFromHand","owner":"card_event_saar_probe_C","params":[{"name":"canIt","type":"bool","direction":"out"},{"name":"reason","type":"FString","direction":"out"},{"name":"reasonParam1","type":"FString","direction":"out"},{"name":"reasonParam2","type":"FString","direction":"out"},{"name":"targetedCard","type":"UBaseCardObject*","direction":"out"}],"pure":false,"event":true,"flags":"FUNC_Event | FUNC_Public | FUNC_HasOutParms | FUNC_BlueprintCallable | FUNC_BlueprintEvent","locals":[{"name":"CallFunc_GetTargetedCard_hasTarget","type":"bool","direction":"in"},{"name":"CallFunc_GetTargetedCard_card","type":"UBaseCardObject*","direction":"in"},{"name":"CallFunc_IsUnit_isIt","type":"bool","direction":"in"}],"body":[{"